WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.340
One of the 

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:40.350
fundamental theses of this course is that
in, in modern literature, and in literature

00:00:41.449 --> 00:00:47.250
that isn’t so modern, there’s the notion
of a human-created monster, Frankenstein,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:52.250
right, springs to mind, and at an adequate
time, I mean, Frankenstein was a, a fantasy,

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:58.820
an unconscious fantasy, a dream in, in actuality,
about the potential dangers of unbridled technological

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:05.820
advancement, right, Why is that? Well maybe
it’s something like this, and, and this

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:17.330
is certainly a, a notion that Jung would agree
with, if we don’t develop a moral sense

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:25.810
as conscious and as elaborated as our technological
sense, the fact that we’re increasingly

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:29.740
capable of becoming increasingly powerful
will necessarily do us in, right.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:36.740
The bigger your weapons, the smarter you better
be to control them, and so maybe it is something

00:01:39.189 --> 00:01:44.569
like this, maybe, and this is a strict Jungian
notion, maybe five hundred years ago when

00:01:44.569 --> 00:01:51.139
we started to ratchet up the rate at which
we were developing our technological expertise,

00:01:51.139 --> 00:01:58.139
and left our mythological and religious presuppositions
and conceptions behind as archaic and perhaps

00:01:59.539 --> 00:02:05.109
as predicated on superstition , maybe we need
to spend as much time updating them and bringing

00:02:05.109 --> 00:02:10.000
them into the domain of clear consciousness
and control as we have spent on developing

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.280
our technological sense.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.620
If you were a medieval Christian or an archaic
religious thinker of any sort, your first

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:25.000
presupposition was that the world and the
cosmos existed exactly as they appeared,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.080
which with you or at least your village or
town or country at the center, and certainly

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:35.599
with the earth at the center, and with the
cosmos as a shield around the earth, and with

00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:41.000
the earth itself as the domain of man being
a fundamental attribute of the cosmos.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:48.000
So this is a quotation from Jung, and it’s
one I like a lot because I think it adequately

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:56.739
and succinctly describes the distinction between
the way that modern people think, and the

00:02:56.739 --> 00:03:02.140
way that people think if they’re still ensconced
within a traditional belief form, How totally,

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:07.430
different, did the world appear to medieval
man. For him the earth was eternally fixed

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:12.750
and it rest in the center of the universe
encircled by the course of a sun that solicitously

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.599
bestowed its warmth,

00:03:14.599 --> 00:03:19.909
Despite the fact that pre-empirical people
had to deal with death and disease on a scale

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:24.480
that I think is completely unknown to us,
it doesn’t seem unreasonable to presuppose

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:24.610
that

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:28.189
there was a certain degree of comfort to be
found in a worldview of this sort,

00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:32.450
right, because it appeared at least to the
casual observer that

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:36.260
the cosmos was human centric,

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:42.500
and that the notion that human purpose was
in some way associated with cosmic significance,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:47.909
seemed to be beyond question, at least in
part because there were no theories of reality

00:03:47.909 --> 00:03:51.219
that would compete with that initial preconception.

00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:55.439
Men were all children of God unto the loving
care of the most High, who prepared them for

00:03:55.439 --> 00:03:59.680
eternal blessedness, and all knew exactly
what they should do and how they should conduct

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:06.680
themselves in order to rise from a corruptible
world to an incorruptible and joyous existence.

00:04:06.959 --> 00:04:13.170
Such a life no longer seems real to us, even
in our dreams. Natural science has long ago

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.140
torn this lovely veil to shreds.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.519
how is it that people could think that way

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:21.549
given that it was so wrong and still survive?

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:26.130
Given that in large part we are necessarily
creatures of tradition.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:33.130
How is it that we can sever the ties with
the manner in which our ancestors thought,

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:38.030
and suffer no ill consequences in result?

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:41.970
People think automatically, and, and I think
for good reasons, that

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:47.900
the march of human thought has been unbroken
progress towards increased rationality,

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.150
increased power, increased clarity, but it’s
certainly the case that as a consequence of

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.190
sacrifice of our religious beliefs

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:58.990
and our philosophical beliefs that problems
of meaning have become more paramount for

00:04:58.990 --> 00:04:59.930
the modern person.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:03.319
And then you might ask, well what exactly
are the consequences of that,

00:05:03.319 --> 00:05:09.250
and I think initially the best perspective
to take is one that’s historical.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:16.250
As we’ve moved away from a classical, mystical
or mythological worldview,

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.539
a number of dramatic occurrences have unfolded,

00:05:20.539 --> 00:05:24.190
we’ve become much more technologically powerful,
right.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:29.180
The application of a strict empirical model
designed to abstract out from everyone’s

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:30.000
experience,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.160
those things that are material and constant
have enabled us to produce

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.930
technological implements of extreme power,
right, both for good, at least in principle

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:42.569
with regards to medical advances,

00:05:42.569 --> 00:05:48.590
and also for ill, in terms of our ability
to control weapons of unbelievable destructive

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:49.150
force.

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:54.860
So we’re more powerful. Are we any smarter
or any wiser?

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:59.460
Well I think a casual glance at the history
of the 20th Century would suggest that perhaps

00:05:59.460 --> 00:05:59.949
we’re not.

00:05:59.949 --> 00:06:03.830
I don’t think there’s any indication whatsoever,
although perhaps things have improved in the

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.639
last fifteen years,

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:12.349
that an additional consequence of our capacity
to extract ourselves from our religious modes

00:06:12.349 --> 00:06:13.139
of thought

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:17.610
has been a palpable increase in wisdom or
tolerance or compassion,

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:22.270
or a palpable increase in our ability to understand
explicitly what might constitute the basis

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:27.990
for a suitable and stable state.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:32.720
So you might take, for example, the fact that
the 20th Century has been unbelievably bloody,

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:38.340
right, literally hundreds of millions of people
killed in conflicts of one form or another,

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:42.129
both external, say, in the course of World
War II or Vietnam,

00:06:42.129 --> 00:06:46.240
and internal, in the case of Nazi Germany
or the Soviet Union,

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:51.819
or any of the vast array of countries who
subjected their citizens to terrible internal

00:06:51.819 --> 00:06:52.690
repression

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:55.349
in the name of the maintenance of order.

00:06:55.349 --> 00:06:57.919
I mentioned that Alexander Solzhenitzyn estimated
that

00:06:57.919 --> 00:07:02.610
sixty million people died in the Soviet Union
between 1919 and 1959

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.569
as a consequence of internal repression,

00:07:05.569 --> 00:07:09.819
and his estimates were that perhaps twice
that many died during the Cultural Revolution

00:07:09.819 --> 00:07:13.900
in China.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:20.509
Looking back on the 20th Century, one seem,
one thing seems relatively clear, right.

00:07:20.509 --> 00:07:27.509
Although humanity as a whole has ceased, or
had ceased at least to engage in large scale

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:31.050
religious conflicts,

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:37.750
although they seem to have been making a vicious
comeback in the last five years,

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:44.599
our ability to live together still seems incredibly
compromised by our capacity to engage in ideological

00:07:44.599 --> 00:07:45.680
conflict, right,

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:52.680
and it doesn’t seem to me ridiculous to
presume that the battles between capitalism

00:07:52.819 --> 00:07:54.190
and communism, say,

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:57.020
or between capitalism, communism and fascism

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:03.360
, or even the emergent struggle between fundamentalist
Islam and the West,

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.550
can necessarily be regarded as anything but
extensions of our tendency towards religious

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:09.449
and mythological conflict,

00:08:09.449 --> 00:08:14.659
even though, in principle, systems like communism
and fascism were not predicated on explicitly

00:08:14.659 --> 00:08:17.430
religious presuppositions.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:24.430
Well it seems to me that a logical conclusion
from observations of that sort

00:08:26.599 --> 00:08:31.900
is that even if you eradicate the traditional
trappings of the mythological worldview,

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:36.159
which seems to be what’s happened as a consequence
of our rise in empirical knowledge,

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:41.669
that you don’t eradicate the tendency for
people to formulate groups, belief systems

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:48.230
, around conceptions of ways that you should
behave

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:52.370
that are, at the very least, religious in
structure and action, even if they’re not

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:53.260
religious in name;

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:56.930
I mean I think it’s a very peculiar coincidence,
for example,

00:08:56.930 --> 00:09:01.410
that the Soviet Communists erected at Male
Trinity

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:08.410
Marx, Lenin and Stalin as an apparent replacement
for the masculine trinity

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:15.680
that comprised the essential deities of Orthodox,
Russian Orthodox Christianity.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:18.399
Why would these forms re-emerge so spontaneously,

00:09:18.399 --> 00:09:24.050
and what does it mean that people who regard
themselves as essentially modern and empirical

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:31.050
in their presuppositions seem to be absolutely
susceptible in their fundament to ideological

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:33.110
claims?

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:36.140
Well if you look at the Soviet Union, which
I think is a very instructive case

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:42.920
because the Soviets, the Russians, were really
the last European power to fall prey to the

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:43.529
conflict

00:09:43.529 --> 00:09:47.019
between empiricism and science and religion,

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:50.190
and they didn’t really fall prey to that
until the mid 1800’s

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:54.820
because Russia was a relatively closed society,
relatively illiterate,

00:09:54.820 --> 00:10:00.940
maintaining a medieval structure far past
the time when other European countries had

00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:02.620
abandoned that.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.029
In the 1850’s, a wave of atheism spread
across Russia,

00:10:07.029 --> 00:10:12.610
in some sense as a wave of enlightenment,
but also as a plague,

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:19.610
and figures as powering as Tolstoy

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:25.300
remembered in his memoirs the very day that
he realized

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:31.610
that the empirical discoveries of Western
Europeans had eradicated his ability to believe

00:10:31.610 --> 00:10:33.430
in the Russian Orthodox system.

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:37.230
You lose something like that, what happens?

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:43.480
Well if you believe that mythological thinking
is nothing but superstitious empiricism

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:47.639
and that it’s been replaced entirely by
a more appropriate modern view, then nothing

00:10:47.639 --> 00:10:48.120
happens, right,

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:52.730
it’s all to your benefit to become enlightened,
but if you believe that there’s more to

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:53.860
the story than that,

00:10:53.860 --> 00:11:00.040
and that more traditional, ethical and moral
systems predicated on mythological presuppositions

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:04.440
offer you a map of how to behave and what
to think and how to regulate your emotions

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:05.720
and what to strive for,

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:11.029
none of which can be replaced by a scientific
perspective, then the eradication of a system

00:11:11.029 --> 00:11:12.760
like that leaves a vacuum,

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:16.339
and then the question is, what rushes in to
fill a vacuum?

00:11:16.339 --> 00:11:19.720
Well if you look at the case of the Soviet
Union, it seems quite instructive, doesn’t

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:20.029
it.

00:11:20.029 --> 00:11:25.240
I mean, there’s a thirty-year period where
there’s tremendous intellectual clash,

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:32.240
say between a materialist, an empirical perspective,
and in Russian Orthodox perspective.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:38.459
The Russian Orthodox perspective loses its
attractiveness for the reigning intellectual

00:11:38.459 --> 00:11:39.329
elite

00:11:39.329 --> 00:11:43.540
and the presuppositions of communism which
appear rational by contrast,

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:48.149
but which, by all evidence, were not, rush
in to fill the gap, and

00:11:48.149 --> 00:11:52.550
I want to read you something that Nietzsche
wrote, it’s perhaps the most famous thing

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:53.880
he ever said,

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:57.889
although it’s almost entirely taken out
of context and misquoted, and if not misquoted,

00:11:57.889 --> 00:11:59.589
at least misunderstood,

00:11:59.589 --> 00:12:03.899
because Nietzsche was one of these strange
people who was capable of living 50 or even

00:12:03.899 --> 00:12:05.610
100 years into the future,

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:11.160
and although he was, is, generally regarded
as an enemy of Christianity and superstition,

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:15.139
and was certainly an unbelievably outspoken
of Christian… opponent of Christian traditionalism,

00:12:15.139 --> 00:12:21.360
How similar. Don’t know exactly how similar
but that’s what stories tell you how similar.

